## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of runtime extensibility, plugin architecture, and composition patterns in a practical context.

## Key Concepts
- **Pipeline vs Chain of Responsibility:** ordered transformation versus conditional delegation
- Immutable registries for thread-safety
- Per-item isolation and error containment across stages

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Formatter {
  transform(line: string): string;
}

class Registry {
  private map = new Map<string, () => Formatter>();

  register(name: string, factory: () => Formatter): void {
    this.map.set(name, factory);
  }

  create(name: string): Formatter | undefined {
    const factory = this.map.get(name);
    return factory ? factory() : undefined;
  }
}

class Pipeline {
  private formatters: Formatter[] = [];

  use(formatter: Formatter): this {
    this.formatters.push(formatter);
    return this;
  }

  execute(line: string): string {
    return this.formatters.reduce(
      (acc, formatter) => formatter.transform(acc),
      line
    );
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('timestamp', () => ({
  transform: (line) => `[${Date.now()}] ${line}`
}));
registry.register('emoji', () => ({
  transform: (line) => `${line} 🪵`
}));

const pipeline = new Pipeline();
pipeline.use(registry.create('timestamp')!);
pipeline.use(registry.create('emoji')!);

console.log(pipeline.execute('log message'));
// Output: [1712345678900] log message 🪵
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to combine the Chain of Responsibility with a registry-driven plugin model, enabling runtime composition and reordering of middleware without touching route code.

## Key Concepts

- Chain of Responsibility
- Registry / Plugin architecture
- Dynamic composition and ordering
- Context mutation versus immutability

## Code Example

```typescript
type Context = { [k: string]: any };
type Next = () => Promise<void>;
type Middleware = (ctx: Context, next: Next) => Promise<void>;

class Registry {
  private store = new Map<string, Middleware[]>();
  register(name: string, mw: Middleware) {
    const arr = this.store.get(name) ?? [];
    arr.push(mw); this.store.set(name, arr);
  }
  get(name: string): Middleware[] | undefined {
    return this.store.get(name);
  }
}

function compose(list: Middleware[]): Middleware {
  return async (ctx, next) => {
    let i = -1;
    const dispatch = async (idx: number) => {
      if (idx < 0) return next();
      const mw = list[idx];
      if (!mw) return next();
      await mw(ctx, () => dispatch(idx + 1));
    };
    await dispatch(0);
  };
}
```

